    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d973">

                <pb facs="1231" n="1219" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg1219"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860h.01/4–1245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-04-12T20:00:00-04:00">April 12, 1945—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>849. Reurtel 1131, April 11.<anchor n="55" xml:id="d973-f1"/> Six members of the
                    Subasic Government at London are included in the present Cabinet. Two of them
                    were Tito appointees after the negotiations at Vis last summer<anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d973-f2"/> and one, Kosanovic, had been the chief Tito protagonist
                    in the United States.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d973-f3"/> The remaining three at
                    the time of the move to Belgrade were Subasic, Sutej and Grol, the latter two
                    being thus the only real representatives of elements not coordinated with the
                    Tito regime, in a <pb facs="1232" n="1220" xml:id="pg1220"/> “United Government”
                    of 28 members. Grol is vice premier, but without other portfolio and Sutej is
                    one of the two Ministers without portfolio.</p>
                <p>Of the 22 appointed at Belgrade 9 are communists, 11 are Partisans (possibly
                    communists and probably communist sympathizers). All of the Partisans were
                    Ministers in Tito’s Avnoj. The remaining two are regarded as Tito supporters,
                    though nominally one is an Agrarian and one a Republican. Of the three Regents
                    two are Partisans.</p>
                <p>The weighting of the present Government is thus apparent. Sutej and Grol are the
                    only members willing to take an independent line on occasion and, as noted
                    above, neither of them holds a portfolio.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d973-f1" n="55" xml:id="d973-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in this telegram, Ambassador Harriman asked the Department for a
                    description and analysis of the Yugoslav provisional government
                    (860c.01/4–1145).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d973-f2" n="56" xml:id="d973-f2-content">
                <p>For Subasich’s account of these negotiations with Marshal Tito, see telegram
                    Yugoslav Series No. 2, July 4, 1944, from London, <ref
                        target="frus1944v04#pg_1384">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1944, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 1384</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d973-f3" n="57" xml:id="d973-f3-content">
                <p>Kosanovich was a former member of the Yugoslav Information Center in New
                    York.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
